[Music]  who were the alchemists  were they magicians concocting elixirs  and potions  were they occultist mystics with access  to secret knowledge  passed down from ancient times or were  they charlatans  tricking gullible people into believing  they could bestow riches and  immortality for the right price well  none of the above really though all of  those descriptions do carry a kernel of  truth  alchemy was a proto-scientific system  that sought to understand  transform and purify natural materials  it started in egypt when exactly no one  knows for sure  the details of egyptian alchemy are also  unknown to us  as most of the alchemical texts from  egypt have been lost  from egypt the system spread around the  old world  through china india the middle east and  europe because of this  alchemy was a diverse system changing  from location to location  as well as changing through time  different alchemists focused on  different things  in china alchemy was tied to taoism and  by extension traditional chinese  medicine  indian alchemists also focused on  medicinal alchemy  while those in the west and middle east  had different goals those of which we  will be focusing on in this video since  the details of western alchemy are known  to modern scholars  the problem with western alchemy is that  these alchemists liked to write down  all their thoughts theories and  experiments in strange codes and  creative analogies  this practice is the reason modern  scholars are still unsure exactly  of what alchemists were up to these  symbols and codes connected alchemy with  astrology  many astrological symbols and ideas were  also used in alchemical texts  seven planets for example corresponded  to seven metals the sun stood for gold  the moon for silver iron for mars tin  for jupiter  copper for venus lead for saturn and  mercury well  obviously for mercury alchemists also  subscribed to the aristotelian idea of  the four  classical elements earth fire air and  water  they eventually added three more  substances to that list  mercury sulfur and salt which they  believed were combined  to create compounds such as metals and  even the bodies of living things  all of this secrecy allegory and  mystical thought contributed  the modern view of alchemists as  sorcerers or magicians  whether alchemists used this magical  language to simply hide away their  knowledge  or if they really believed in the  spiritual and supernatural  connections to their work remains to be  seen more than likely it was a mixture  of both  okay so what do we know in general  alchemy had three main goals  the first was the transmutation of base  metals like lead  into precious metals like gold this  process was called chrysopia the second  was the manufacturer of the elixir of  life  able to stave off death as well as cure  all illnesses and  lastly the creation of an alcoholist or  a universal solvent  able to dissolve anything even gold how  they thought they could contain such a  substance eludes me  but i digress all of these goals  eventually got tied to the creation of  the philosopher's stone which we'll get  into in more detail later  it might seem strange to us in the  modern world to think of sciences as  having  tangible goals like this nowadays  sciences are simply the studies of  different natural phenomena  physics has no goals other than the  understanding of the fabric and forces  that make up our universe  obviously physics as well as all other  sciences  have plenty of practical application but  it would be strange to say that the  point of physics  was to make quantum computers so how is  it that alchemists thought these goals  were possible  did they come out of nowhere well  alchemy in the beginning didn't have  these exact goals  alchemists came from the likes of  metallurgists doctors and natural  philosophers that were simply trying to  understand the world around them  but alchemists as well as all  pre-scientific people  relied just as much if not more on the  wisdom of the past as they did on  observation  if something was written down in a book  by a smart person from long ago  those ideas would usually be taken for  granted so  alchemists were synthesizing both their  own observations and experiments  with the ideas of the likes of aristotle  and plato as well as from religious or  philosophical traditions like  christianity a good example of this  synthesis  is the transmutation of lead into gold  it seems like a far-fetched idea to us  but when metallurgists would heat metal  ore the ore would change color  and different compounds would appear in  the process we know now  that these reactions are because of the  impurities found in raw ore but  alchemists at the time  thought it was evidence of the  transformation of the material and of  course  chemical transmutation is a real and  natural phenomenon  and we see the transformation of things  in nature all the time it's no wonder  really that alchemists thought there  would be a way of using this  transformative aspect of nature to their  advantage  chrysopea was also seen as a mirror unto  the process of christian salvation  the transformation of lead into gold  mirrored the purification of a sinner  through god's grace  okay so what about this philosopher's  stone the process of creating the stone  was  varied different texts gave slightly  different instructions  with different alchemists having varied  approaches  in general it was achieved through  heating various elements together  until the combination of these elements  would go through a number of color  changes  the material would first turn black then  white then yellow  red and finally at least in theory it  would become the philosopher's stone  this stone could then be used to turn  base metals into gold by heating some of  it with said base metal  it could also be ingested by making it  into a powder and dissolving it into  wine  that process would create the elixir of  life many alchemists especially in the  late medieval period as well as in the  renaissance were obsessed with creating  the stone  the creation of it was known as the  magnum opus or  great work and it became the main goal  of later alchemists  legends would often pop up about someone  successfully creating the stone  and these stories kept the dream alive  for future alchemists  many famous scientists and philosophers  were alchemists  isaac newton the inventor of calculus  was also an  alchemist in his spare time tycho brahi  the famous astronomer was an alchemist  and robert boyle known as the father of  chemistry  was originally an alchemist alchemy was  an extremely important precursor to the  sciences of chemistry and medicine  and though it failed to reach its aims  it made very important discoveries and  inventions  chinese alchemists are credited with the  creation of gunpowder  and western alchemists discovered  sulfuric acid as well as other important  chemical compounds  alchemists made important contributions  to metallurgy ceramics glass making and  medicine  in the 15th century alchemy had grown a  bad reputation  writer chaucer and others mocked  alchemists as liars and quacks  pope john xxii as well as king henry iv  of england  made alchemy illegal alchemy was revived  in the renaissance  but in the 18th century after the  scientific revolution  alchemy once again gained a reputation  of charlatanism  after that alchemy slowly declined  modern day alchemists  who yes do exist move mostly within  esoteric or new age  circles because of this the modern view  of alchemy is generally negative  or at least it's not taken very  seriously which is a real shame  because while the science of alchemy has  been widely discredited  it should be looked on at least  historically with a bit more respect  thanks for watching  you

